# Citation metadata, in the Citation File Format (https://citation-file-format.org).
# GitHub reads this and shows a "Cite this repository" button.  Zenodo reads
# .zenodo.json instead when minting a DOI from a release, and ignores this file
# while that one exists; check_citation.py holds the fields the two share
# equal, and the citation workflow runs it on every push, pull request, and
# tag.  The steps of a release are in README.md, under "Releasing".
#
####   RELEASE CHECKLIST.  These are the steps that go wrong inside this file.
####
####   1. Zenodo is switched on for this repository.  It archives releases
####      made after it is switched on and does not reach back for earlier
####      ones.
####   2. On each tagged release, bump `version` and `date-released` to match,
####      and in .zenodo.json bump `version`, the version in `title`, and the
####      tag in the GitHub tree link.  This file is where mLOSweb's version
####      is written; check_citation.py holds all four equal to `version`
####      here, and in a tag build holds the tag equal to `v` plus `version`,
####      the tag the tree link names.
####   3. Check the date in UTC (`TZ=UTC date +%F`) before tagging.  GitHub
####      stamps tags in UTC and Zenodo dates records in Geneva local time, so
####      an evening tag on the United States west coast lands on the next day
####      in both of them and disagrees with what is written here.
####   4. For `doi:`, use Zenodo's CONCEPT DOI, the version-independent one
####      that always resolves to the newest release, not the DOI of a single
####      release.  A release's own DOI does not exist until that release is
####      archived, by which point this file is already inside it, so it could
####      only ever name the release before.  The concept DOI is stable, and
####      `version` above is what says which release was actually run.  There
####      is therefore no `doi:` field until the release after the first
####      archived one.
####   5. AFTER publishing a release, check the Zenodo record against
####      .zenodo.json: title `mLOSweb (Version X.Y.Z)`, version `X.Y.Z`
####      without a `v`, the related works, and the two references.  Without
####      that file Zenodo takes the version from the tag name, and a related
####      work or reference entered on one version's record does not carry to
####      the next.  A field name Zenodo does not know is dropped without
####      notice, so check in the Zenodo interface at the time.  Zenodo fails
####      the archive on a .zenodo.json it cannot read; check_citation.py
####      checks that the file parses and uses only the field and relation
####      names Zenodo accepts.
#
####   A related work or reference is added, changed, or removed in
####   .zenodo.json before tagging, not on the Zenodo record: Zenodo rebuilds
####   each version's record from that file, so an edit made on the record is
####   lost at the next release.
#
####   The related works name mLOS and ShelterDataPrep by their CONCEPT DOIs,
####   10.5281/zenodo.22083814 and 10.5281/zenodo.22051338, with the relation
####   "requires": the page cannot run without them, and which version it runs
####   is pins.py's business, so the relation stays the same across releases.
####   Pyodide and webR are "requires" for the same reason.  The demo exports'
####   deposits and the mLOS paper are "references", and the live page is
####   what the record "is source of".
#
####   .zenodo.json names no license, so Zenodo takes the record's from the
####   repository's LICENSE file.

cff-version: 1.2.0
message: >-
  If you use this software, please cite it with the version you ran.  Results
  computed with it come from mLOS and ShelterDataPrep, which ran unchanged
  inside the page, so also cite those two at the versions the page's footer
  reports.
title: "mLOSweb"
abstract: >-
  A web page that runs ShelterDataPrep and mLOS inside the browser, for animal
  shelters without an R or Python installation.  The user loads an export of
  the shelter's stays, as a CSV file or an Excel workbook, answers a few
  questions about it, and gets the mLOS plots, tables, workbook, and slide
  deck.  The data is not uploaded anywhere.  ShelterDataPrep runs in Pyodide
  (Python compiled to WebAssembly) and mLOS in webR (R compiled to
  WebAssembly).  The page runs a subset of mLOS: it leaves out the Weibull
  regression and the confidence bounds of the conditional outcome curves, and
  compares at most four periods, six intake groups, and six animal groups.
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Mavrovouniotis"
    given-names: "Michael Loizos"
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0009-0009-9896-2761"
version: "0.1.0"
date-released: "2026-11-10"
license: MIT
repository-code: "https://github.com/Shelter-Data-Analysis/mLOSweb"
url: "https://shelter-data-analysis.github.io/mLOSweb/"
keywords:
  - animal shelter
  - length of stay
  - survival analysis
  - WebAssembly
  - webR
  - Pyodide

# What the page runs and the demo exports it offers.  Software is cited by its
# concept DOI, with the version pins.py names in the notes; data by its
# version DOI, as the deposit that holds the exact bytes the build checks.
# If a record's own title or author list changes, change it here too.
